    </header>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
      <section class="mb-12">
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Walk into any beverage warehouse, FMCG distributor, or contract logistics yard in Shah Alam and you will see them: thin sheets of corrugated cardboard sandwiched between every layer of cartons on a pallet. Layer pads — also called interleaving sheets, slip sheets, or pallet divider boards — are one of the cheapest insurance policies in supply chain. A few cents per sheet stops the corner-crush damage that costs Malaysian warehouses tens of thousands of ringgit per year. Quinocycle supplies these sheets from our Balakong cutting line in standard and custom sizes.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="font-headline text-2xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-6">Why Layer Pads Matter in Real Warehouses</h2>
        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Spreading Point Load</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Carton corners are the strongest point of a box; the carton walls in between are not. Without a layer pad, the corner of an upper carton presses directly into the wall of a lower carton, eventually crushing it. A layer pad spreads that load across the full surface area, transferring weight to the strong carton corners below. This single change cuts crush damage by 40-60% in our customers' before-and-after data.</p>

        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Stabilising Multi-SKU Stacks</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Mixed-SKU pallets (different product sizes per layer) wobble during forklift movement and sea freight. A layer pad gives the next layer a flat, predictable base regardless of what's underneath. Combined with stretch wrap, this stops the leaning Pisa-tower problem that ruins a third of mixed-pallet loads.</p>

        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Protecting Branded & Printed Cartons</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Premium FMCG brands invest heavily in printed shipper cartons. A layer pad keeps tape residue, scuffs, and ink transfer from one layer marking the artwork on the next. For retail-ready packaging arriving direct on a Tesco or AEON shelf, this matters.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="font-headline text-2xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-6">Sizing & Thickness Guide</h2>
        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Standard Malaysian Pallet Sizes</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Most domestic Malaysian warehouses run 1100x1100mm square pallets. Export-oriented operations use 1200x1000mm Euro-style. We stock both as off-the-shelf cuts. Custom dimensions cut from full sheets within 5 working days.</p>

        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Choosing the Right Flute</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">3-ply C-flute is the workhorse — fine for layer loads under 200kg. 5-ply double-wall (BC flute) handles 200-500kg layers including beverages, cans, and ceramic tiles. Above 500kg per layer (industrial chemicals, machinery parts), step up to honeycomb panels.</p>

        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Top Caps & Slip Sheets</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">A top cap (sheet over the top layer) is added to protect cartons from the binding straps and stretch wrap. A slip sheet (sheet under the bottom layer, flush with the pallet deck) replaces the pallet itself in some export lanes — saving cubic-metre charges and ISPM-15 fumigation cost. Ask us about combined orders.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="font-headline text-2xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-6">Industries That Run on Layer Pads</h2>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Beverage and FMCG bottlers in Shah Alam and Klang use them between every layer of canned drinks. Ceramic-tile distributors in Subang stack tiles 8 layers high — without pads the bottom tiles fracture under transit vibration. Food canners need pads between cans to prevent dent transfer. Even paper merchants put pads between paper reels to prevent dirt and scuff. If you run pallet-based distribution, you are likely under-using layer pads.</p>
      </section>

      <div class="bg-surface-variant/50 border border-outline-variant/20 rounded-lg p-8 my-12">
        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-6">Layer Pad Spec Quick Reference</h3>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          <div>
            <p class="font-label text-primary font-bold text-sm mb-2">SQUARE PALLET STANDARD</p>
